+## 17. X2 T1: exec-equivalent job control for `run` (2026-10-10)
+
+Task X2 T1 (`TASK.md`): in five situations `heimdall-proto run -- cmd` behaved differently from running `cmd`
+directly (plain exec) under a job-control shell or a third party's stops. Only `src/runner.rs` (Unix `run`)
+changed; Windows `run`, the forwarding rules, the exit-status mapping and the meta file are unchanged.
+
+### Baseline → outcome (both checked against a plain-exec control in the same environment)
+
+| Scenario | Before (round-3c code) | Cause | Now |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| S1 non-shell parent, no tty on stdio, own non-orphaned group; third party SIGSTOP/SIGTSTP to the child, 0.5 s, SIGCONT to the child | heimdall-proto never exits (the §9.5 Rust deviation): it mirrored the stop onto itself, and a stopped process cannot see the child being continued | the stop was mirrored whether or not any job-control shell was watching | mirrored only when a job-control shell can be watching; here heimdall-proto keeps waiting, the child completes, exit 7 about 1.2 s after SIGCONT, child reaped |
+| S2 foreground job; third party stops then continues the child | already conformed, deviation (b): notice, `$?` 128+N, job listed stopped until `fg`, `fg` ends it with the child's status | — | unchanged (b) |
+| S3 child stops itself (SIGTSTP); third party SIGCONT to the launcher PID only | child stayed stopped; bash 5.2/zsh then listed the job as running (the §15 "unprobed residual") and on Linux bash `fg` did not finish it | after its mirrored stop, heimdall-proto never continued the child | after a mirrored stop, if no continue reached the child within 10 ms, heimdall-proto sends it SIGCONT; the child's background read then stops it with SIGTTIN, which is mirrored as SIGTTIN (the job is listed stopped, "tty input"); `fg` → the child reads the line, `$?` 9 |
+| S4 nested `bash -i` under `run`, `suspend`, `fg`, `exit 3` | after `fg` the nested bash never answered | the nested bash runs in its own process group and held the terminal; `fg` gave the terminal to heimdall-proto's group and continued only that group | on resume heimdall-proto gives the terminal back to the child's group (only when its own group holds it and the child's group held it before the stop) and continues that group; the nested bash answers, `$?` 3 |
+| S5 `--meta-file`, SIGSTOP (self or third party) | already conformed | — | unchanged: notice, `$?` 128+SIGSTOP, child and launcher stay stopped until `fg`, `$?` 0, meta `CHILD_EXITED` `{"launched": true, "exit_code": 0, "signal": null}` |
+
+### Mechanism (`src/runner.rs`: `job_terminal`, `handle_child_stop`, `resume_child`)
+- **When to mirror.** On a child stop report, heimdall-proto consumes the report and opens `/dev/tty`
+  (read-only, `O_NOCTTY|O_NONBLOCK`, query only). It mirrors only if that succeeds and stdin, stdout or stderr is
+  that terminal (`tcgetsid` = own session) or heimdall-proto's group is the terminal's foreground group. Otherwise
+  no job-control shell reports heimdall-proto's stops, so it just keeps waiting (S1). The §15 grace (200 ms with no
+  continue/exit report, Q18) and the choice of signal (same signal; SIGSTOP when not TSTP/TTIN/TTOU or inherited
+  as ignored) are unchanged. Only heimdall-proto's own PID is stopped (K6a).
+- **After the mirrored stop.** `kill(getpid(), sig)` returns once heimdall-proto has been continued. It then waits
+  up to 10 ms for the continue that `fg`/`bg` sends to the whole group to show up for the child. Then:
+  if the child's group differs from heimdall-proto's, held the terminal before the stop, and heimdall-proto's group
+  holds it now, `tcsetpgrp` gives it back to the child's group (S4); if no continue reached the child, heimdall-proto
+  continues it (`kill(child, SIGCONT)`, or `killpg` of the child's own group when the child left heimdall-proto's
+  group) (S3, S4). With `fg`/`bg` on the shared group nothing extra is sent.
+- **Fresh stops.** A stop within 1 s after heimdall-proto itself continued the child is mirrored after 20 ms
+  instead of 200 ms: heimdall-proto kept running since that SIGCONT, so the report cannot be stale in the Q18 way,
+  and the time in which the shell lists the job as running while the child is stopped stays short (S3).
